Chapter: DiagMsc
  cites Works chapter GopUsbMsc
  cites Diag chapter DiagStage

 We say:

Section: The Question

 Does a bulk TRB whose data buffer crosses a 64 KB boundary return the same
 bytes as one that does not? The seed read issues about thirty crossing TRBs
 on an ordinary boot, so a controller that mishandles the crossing corrupts
 the artifact we booted from and says nothing.

 This is the lift of `MscAlignProbe` (flown 2026-08-05, banked in
 `HardwareSitting.md` as `msc-align.img`). It READS and never writes, so it
 sits in the ladder before every stage that can wedge the box; the sink's
 write is the wedge risk and is deferred past this row on purpose.

Section: The Buffers

 `xa` starts 1024 bytes below a 64 KB boundary, so a 32 KB read from it
 crosses. `al` is the boundary itself, so an equal read from it cannot. Both
 read the SAME LBA, so the bytes must be identical and any difference is the
 controller.

  dma-span : Integer = 262144
  dma-cross-off : Integer = 64512
  dma-sectors : Integer = 64
  dma-lba : Integer = 0

  dma-chk : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dma-chk (a) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= 64 then acc
    else dma-chk a (i + 1) (acc + peek-32 a (i * 512))

Section: The Liveness Control

 WITHOUT THIS ROW THE TWO ABOVE PROVE NOTHING. A driver that answers from a
 stale buffer, or one that reports success without transferring, returns
 equal checksums for both reads and looks perfect. So a third read is issued
 past the end of the medium, where the only correct answer is failure: if it
 SUCCEEDS the instrument is not measuring the device and the verdict says so
 rather than reporting a pass (L-FALSIF, and the same shape as the probe's
 own `LIVENESS` line).

 IT GOES THROUGH `msc-read10` AND NOT `msc-read-into`, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE
 DIFFERENCE BETWEEN A PROBE AND A REGRESSION. `msc-read-into` treats a failed
 chunk as a fault to recover from: it calls `msc-retry-chunk`, which issues a
 BULK-ONLY MASS STORAGE RESET on the device and then retries, and it writes
 `msc-cell-retry` on the way. A probe whose EXPECTED answer is failure must
 not do either. The first version of this stage used `msc-read-into` and so
 reset the bus mid-ladder on every single run and left `msc-retry-failed` in
 the cell that `sink-revived` and `sink-dies` assert on. `msc-read10` stamps
 one CBW and answers a Boolean.

 FOUND WHILE WRONGLY BLAMING IT FOR SOMETHING ELSE, and the correction is
 worth as much as the defect. Three arms went red on the first full
 rehearsal after this stage landed (esp-cfg, nic-invisible, b3-short) and I
 named this mechanism as the cause on the strength of it explaining them.
 blu's control settled it the other way: same tree, same config, only blu's
 own `DiagAsde` change reverted, and all three came back green, so this stage
 is not sufficient to produce them. A mechanism that explains a symptom is
 not its cause until something MOVES the symptom (L-MECHANISM). The reset is
 a real defect and is fixed here on its own merits, not as that cause.

 ONE SECTOR, not sixty-four, so the probe cannot fail for the wrong reason.
 `msc-read-into` splits a request at the configured transfer chunk and
 `msc-read10` does not, so a sixty-four sector command issued directly would
 be over the cap wherever `sink chunk=N` is set and could be refused for the
 size rather than the address. A single sector past the end is refused for
 the address on any device, which is the whole question.

 THE LESSON THAT GENERALISES: "reads only" is not a risk class. What matters
 is what the driver does when the read FAILS, and a probe built to provoke a
 failure is exactly the caller that finds out.

  dma-bad-lba : MscDisk -> Integer
  dma-bad-lba (d) = (d.md-sectors) + 16

Section: Text

  dma-yn : Integer -> Text
  dma-yn (v) = if v == 0 then "n" else "y"

  dma-okn : Boolean -> Text
  dma-okn (b) = if b then "y" else "n"

Section: The Stage

 `no-disk` is honest rather than green: a box whose boot medium is AHCI,
 NVMe or IDE has no USB mass-storage disk to ask, and this row cannot tell
 that apart from a bring-up that failed to publish. Both are `no-disk` and
 the verdict says the question was not asked, because inventing a
 distinction the cells do not carry is how a row starts lying (L-STATES).

  dma-states : List Text = ["ok", "mismatch", "crossing-failed", "aligned-failed", "VACUOUS", "no-disk"]

  dma-run : DiagCtx -> DiagResult
  dma-run (c) =
    let d = msc-published
    in if d.md-ok == False then diag-result "no-disk" diag-col-warn ["no USB mass-storage disk published; the 64K crossing was not asked"] ["msc-align: no-disk, question not asked"]
    else let hp = __heap-save
    in let adv = __heap-advance dma-span
    in let al = hp + (65536 - int-mod hp 65536)
    in let xa = al + dma-cross-off
    in let ra = msc-read-into d dma-lba dma-sectors al
    in let cha = dma-chk al 0 0
    in let rc = msc-read-into d dma-lba dma-sectors xa
    in let chc = dma-chk xa 0 0
    in let ro = if msc-read10 d (dma-bad-lba d) 1 al then 1 else 0
    in let rs = __heap-restore hp
    in let st = if ro /= 0 then "VACUOUS"
                else if ra == 0 then "aligned-failed"
                else if rc == 0 then "crossing-failed"
                else if cha /= chc then "mismatch"
                else "ok"
    in let colour = if st == "ok" then diag-col-good
                    else if st == "no-disk" then diag-col-warn
                    else diag-col-bad
    in let g1 = "sectors=" & show (d.md-sectors) & " aligned=" & diag-hex8 al & " off=" & show (int-mod al 65536) & " ok=" & dma-yn ra & " chk=" & diag-hex8 cha
    in let g2 = "crossing=" & diag-hex8 xa & " off=" & show (int-mod xa 65536) & " ok=" & dma-yn rc & " chk=" & diag-hex8 chc & " identical=" & dma-yn (if cha == chc then 1 else 0)
    in let g3 = "liveness lba=" & show (dma-bad-lba d) & " ok=" & dma-yn ro & " (must be n, or the rows above prove nothing)"
    in diag-result st colour [g1, g2, g3] [g1, g2, g3]
